* Bose-Einstein: \(\propto 1/\exp(m_{T}/T_{\rm BE}-1)\)
* \(p_{T}\)-exponential: \(\propto\exp(-p_{T}/T_{p_{T}})\)
* \(m_{T}\)-exponential: \(\propto\exp(-m_{T}/T_{m_{T}})\)
* Boltzmann: \(\propto m_{T}\exp(-m_{T}/T_{B})\)
* Double-exponential: \(A\,e^{-p_{T}^{2}/T_{1}^{2}}+B\,e^{-p_{T}^{2}/T_{2}^{2}}\)

Where \(m_{T}\) is the transverse mass of the particle under consideration, \(T_{BE}\) is the Bose-Einstein temperature, which is a fit parameter. \(T_{p_{T}}\) and \(T_{m_{T}}\) are also the temperatures obtained from fitting in the respective fit functions. \(A\), \(B\), \(T_{1}\) and \(T_{2}\) are all fit parameters.

Apart from these systematic errors for the case of \(p_{T}\) spectra, an additional error of 5\(\%\) is added in quadrature due to detector tracking efficiency and acceptance [6]. The procedure of determination of pion feed-down correction and the proton background fraction also contribute to the systematic uncertainty; however, the former is negligible and the latter is significant only at low \(p_{T}\). The proton background fraction contributes about 5-6% systematic error in the proton spectra at low \(p_{T}\). All the sources of systematic errors are added in quadrature with the statistical error, and are tabulated in Table 3.7. The total systematic errors on pion, kaon and proton yields are \(10\%\), \(10\%\) and \(12\%\), respectively.

