canonical and grand canonical ensemble are shown. It can be well observed from the figure that, the deviation of the particle yields from model predictions to that obtained in the experiment are least with the choice of grand canonical ensemble. This suggests that better description of the particle yields is provided by grand canonical ensemble even for the small system of p+p collisions similar to heavy-ions [6, 43]. The multi-strange particles like \(\Xi\), \(\Omega\) and especially the hidden strange particle like \(\phi\) show maximum value of deviation especially in strange canonical and canonical ensemble. This might suggest that the freeze-out mechanism for these multi-strange and hidden strange particles is more involved and can not be explained alone in the simple 1CFO scheme.

In Fig. 5.2, the results on all the chemical freeze-out parameters \(T_{ch}\), \(\mu_{B}\), \(\gamma_{S}\), \(R\) and \(\chi^{2}/\)ndf obtained from the thermal model fit in GCE, SCE and CE are presented. The \(\chi^{2}/\)ndf represents the fit quality with \(ndf=\) number of data points \(-\) number of free parameters. The \(\chi^{2}/ndf\) values plotted in the Fig. 5.2 agrees with our above remark that the grand canonical ensemble (GCE) describes the data best at all energies as it stays between \(2-3\) for RHIC and higher energies while at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=17.3\) GeV the fit worsens to \(\chi^{2}/ndf\sim 5\). The increase in the value of \(\chi^{2}/\)ndf from RHIC to LHC energies can also be interpreted in terms of more precise measurements done at LHC than RHIC. This reduces, the error values on the particle yields resulting in the value of \(\chi^{2}/\)ndf to go up. We can see that the freeze-out temperature \(T\) monotonically decreases from 170 MeV at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=\) 17.3 GeV to \(\sim 150\) MeV at 7 TeV. \(\mu_{B}\) shows a sharp fall from \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=17.3\) GeV to \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV beyond which it hovers around zero as in heavy ion collisions (HICs). The value of \(\gamma_{S}\) monotonically rises from \(0.3\) to \(0.8\) between SPS and LHC energies. The fireball radius rises from \(\sim 1.3\) fm at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV to \(\sim 1.7\) fm at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=7\) TeV. The canonical ensemble provides the worst fit with \(\chi^{2}/ndf\geq 10\). The fitted temperature, in this case comes out to be greater than 170 MeV for all the energies. The fits from strangeness canonical ensemble also do not seem to be better as it has \(\chi^{2}/ndf\geq 5\) for all the energies.

Previously, we have observed that the THERMUS model [21] does not estimate the yield of \(\phi\) and hence we need to look systematic effect of the yield of \(\phi\) on the values of extracted freeze-out parameters. To have a better understanding of the effect of the particle \(\phi\), we have extracted the chemical freeze-out parameters in three different ensembles excluding the yield of \(\phi\) in the thermal model fit. The \(T_{ch}\), \(\mu_{B}\), \(\gamma_{S}\), \(R\) and \(\chi^{2}/\)ndf so obtained are shown in Fig. 5.3. We can observe that excluding \(\phi\) from the thermal model fit slightly improves the value of \(\chi^{2}/ndf\) especially at energies above \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV. However, excluding \(\phi\) from the thermal model fit has no significant effect on the extracted chemical freeze-out parameters. So, we do not gain much here, except the only plus point comes is the slightly better value of \(\chi^{2}/\)ndf.

We have analyzed the data from the small system of p+p collisions in the 2CFO scheme as well. The strangeness canonical ensemble in 2CFO provide a better fit with reference to \(\chi^{2}/ndf\) than 1CFO, however, the strange freeze-out temperatures turn out to be large (\(>180\) MeV). The 2CFO fits in the grand canonical ensemble yield reasonable temperatures. In Fig. 5.4, we have presented the comparison between 1CFO and 2CFO schemes in the GCE. The 1CFO parameters almost agree with the non-strange parameters of 2CFO. Unlike to HICs, where the 2CFO scheme provides a much better description of the hadron yields than 1CFO [16], here in p+p collisions we find the \(\chi^{2}/ndf\) is similar and one does not gain much by introducing two additional parameters in 2CFO compared to \(\gamma_{S}\) included in 1CFO. The other noticeable dif

